==English==
{{was wotd|2018|October|21}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|dum|tatelen}}, {{m|dum|tateren||to babble, chatter}}, originally {{glossary|imitative}}.<ref>{{R:Oxford Dictionaries Online}}</ref> The word is cognate with {{cog|nds|tateln}}, {{m|nds|täteln||to cackle, gabble}}.<ref>{{R:Oxford English Dictionary|entry=Tattle, sb.|volume=IX|part=2|page=111|column=2}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈtæt(ə)l/|lang=en}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|/ˈtætl̩/|/-ɾl̩/|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|tat|tle|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|tattl}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[chatter#Verb|chatter]]; to [[gossip#Verb|gossip]].
#* '''1599''', [[w:William Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]], ''[[w:Much Ado About Nothing|Much Ado About Nothing]]'', [[s:Much Ado About Nothing (Shakespeare)|Act 2 Scene 1]]
#*: He were an excellent man that were made just in the mid-way between him and Benedick: the one is too like an image, and says nothing; and the other too like my lady's eldest son, evermore '''tattling'''.
#* Dryden
#*: the '''tattling''' quality of age, which is always narrative
# {{senseid|en|to report others' wrongdoings or violations}}{{lb|en|intransitive|Canada|US|pejorative}} ''Often said of children'': to [[report#Verb|report]] [[incriminating#Adjective|incriminating]] [[information]] about another person, or a person's [[wrongdoing]]; to [[tell on]] somebody. {{defdate|from late 15th c.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To [[speak]] like a [[baby#Noun|baby]] or [[young#Adjective|young]] [[child]]; to [[babble#Verb|babble]], to [[prattle]]; to speak [[haltingly]]; to [[stutter#Verb|stutter]].
#* Attested in 1481 in William Caxton's translation of “[[w:Reynard|The History of Reynard the Fox]]”

